1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,200
A cyclic arbitrage normally reconciles three
inconsistent pools by executing three swaps.

2
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:14,850
Even when the profit is reinjected, the EVM
still pays to replay every leg.

3
00:00:14,850 --> 00:00:22,500
ARBFOLD asks whether cooperating v4 pools can
settle the equivalent state directly.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,150
This controlled v0.1 benchmark starts both paths
from the same pool state.

5
00:00:30,150 --> 00:00:39,600
At canonical 100k, the iterative reference
executes two cyclic arbitrage rounds.

6
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:48,150
That means six arbitrage swaps and two
profit reinjections after the user swap.

7
00:00:48,150 --> 00:00:58,050
One ARBFOLD fold() call instead processes
two runtime-checked direct settlement rounds.

8
00:00:58,050 --> 00:01:05,250
At 100k: 544,219 gas versus 375,171.
ARBFOLD uses 31.06% less in this benchmark.

9
00:01:05,250 --> 00:01:12,450
The user output is exactly equal in both paths.

10
00:01:12,450 --> 00:01:20,550
The fixed external-recipient reward is also
exactly equal.

11
00:01:20,550 --> 00:01:28,200
The six final reserves are equivalent within
the measured one-wei tolerance.

12
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:35,850
At 25k: 409,402 gas versus 329,777.

13
00:01:35,850 --> 00:01:43,500
That is 19.45% less gas, removing the
historical v0 regression at this workload.

14
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:51,600
The dense sweep also shows the boundary:
1k through 4k execute zero fold rounds.

15
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:59,250
Those zero-round calls cost more because there
is no actionable settlement to perform.

16
00:01:59,250 --> 00:02:06,450
From 5k through 200k, 196 of 196 actionable
workloads were cheaper in the tested canonical path.

17
00:02:06,450 --> 00:02:15,000
That is a path-specific experimental result,
not a universal claim that ARBFOLD is always cheaper.

18
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:23,550
The public Unichain Sepolia demo executes the
public v0.1 deployment.

19
00:02:23,550 --> 00:02:32,100
The historical v0 research deployment remains
archived and is not a primary submission link.

20
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:39,750
The public demo uses the official v4 PoolManager
with hook-owned custom-curve pools.

21
00:02:39,750 --> 00:02:47,850
Every virtual reserve remains backed by its
PoolManager ERC-6909 claim balance.

22
00:02:47,850 --> 00:02:55,950
Conservation, reserve bounds and non-decreasing
pool invariants are checked at runtime.

23
00:02:55,950 --> 00:03:03,600
The current suite contains 82 Solidity tests
across default and release profiles.

24
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:11,700
It covers all six paths, stateful invariants,
negative cases and exact PoolManager backing.

25
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:18,450
We preregistered a stronger economic claim:
10% more LP net value.

26
00:03:18,450 --> 00:03:26,550
It failed. The measured improvement was only
0.000287% in that frozen environment.

27
00:03:26,550 --> 00:03:34,650
We preserved the rejected economic result and
all historical v0 benchmark evidence unchanged.

28
00:03:34,650 --> 00:03:42,750
What survived is an execution-gas advantage
in the tested actionable workloads.

29
00:03:42,750 --> 00:03:50,400
Steady-state telemetry gas is not claimed because
it has not been measured across real transactions.

30
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:58,500
ARBFOLD is a fixed three-CPMM research experiment,
not a universal arbitrage detector.

31
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:04,800
It is not audited for production and does not
claim material LP-net uplift.

32
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:12,000
Do not replay every leg.
Settle the equivalent state.
